/**
 * THE OBJECT FAMILY — nine artefacts from the print floor.
 *
 * Flat, two colour: a black line on a lime fill. They are the marks in empty states, the
 * illustrations in feature cards and the glyphs beside section headings. They exist because
 * this direction is not carried by type alone — the reference it comes from pairs restrained
 * typography with a small set of drawn objects, and without them a parchment page with one
 * accent is just a tasteful blank.
 *
 * WHY INLINE SVG AND NOT <img>. Each object reads two CSS variables, so ONE file serves all
 * three grounds instead of needing a light copy and a dark copy:
 *
 *   --eg-icon-ink    the outline. #14140F on parchment and on white, #F5F5EB on the dark
 *                    block — without it the drawing disappears into its own ground.
 *   --eg-icon-void   the interior gaps (the ring inside the hoop, the highlight on the cone).
 *                    Those are real drawing, not background, so they follow whatever surface
 *                    sits behind them.
 *   --eg-icon-fill   the object's body colour — the accent. Tokenised on 2026-08-26 when the
 *                    accent moved from lime to pink and every object kept rendering lime on a
 *                    pink tile. The ink and the voids were variables; the FILL was still a
 *                    literal, which is the whole lesson: a palette is only swappable to the
 *                    depth you actually tokenised it.
 *
 * An <img> can see neither. Set both on the CONTAINER, not on the svg.
 *
 * SCALE: these are ILLUSTRATION, 48px and up. Below roughly 48 the barcode and the swatch fan
 * collapse into mush — measured on a size ramp, not guessed. Phosphor stays the UI icon set
 * for anything smaller.
 *
 * ONE KNOWN IMPURITY: SwatchFan carries a 12-stop gradient. Every stop is the same lime and
 * only the OPACITY varies, so it renders flat and reads as the cards overlapping — but it is
 * the one object that would break if the palette were snapped again by a script that only
 * looks at `fill`. That script does exactly that, which is how this got here unnoticed.
 */
